\section{Shape}{
The shape comes from the output that you give: \code{plotOutput()} gets the
shape of a chart, \code{tableOutput()} gets rows and columns, and
\code{textOutput()} gets lines of text. Use \code{type} to set a different shape.
You must do this for \code{uiOutput()}, because its content is not known
before it arrives.

A \code{plotOutput()} gets the shape of a bar chart. If the chart is of
another kind, give that kind: \code{"line"}, \code{"scatter"}, \code{"area"},
\code{"histogram"}, \code{"pie"} or \code{"heatmap"}. The output cannot tell, because
\code{renderPlot()} decides the kind later, on the server.

A table from a table package gets the shape of that package, found from
its output: \code{DT::DTOutput()} gets \code{"dt"}, \code{reactable::reactableOutput()}
gets \code{"reactable"}, \code{gt::gt_output()} gets \code{"gt"}, and
\code{rhandsontable::rHandsontableOutput()} gets \code{"rhandsontable"}. Each shape
copies the parts of its package, such as the search box of DT. Set \code{rows}
to the number of rows on a page: DT and reactable show 10 by default.
Before gt 1.0.0, \code{gt_output()} did not mark its output as a gt table, so
with an older gt, give \code{type = "gt"}.

A visualisation widget gets the shape of its kind, found from its output
in the same way. A map (leaflet, tmap, mapview, mapdeck and others) gets
\code{"map"}. A network or a diagram (visNetwork, DiagrammeR, networkD3,
collapsibleTree) gets \code{"network"}. timevis gets \code{"timeline"}, wordcloud2
gets \code{"wordcloud"}, and dygraphs gets \code{"line"}. ggmap and a ggplot2 map
draw into a \code{plotOutput()}, so give them \code{type = "map"}.

A plotly, echarts4r or highcharter output can draw any kind of chart. With
no \code{type}, it finds its kind by itself, but only when its first value
arrives in the browser: the spec of the chart names the kind of each
series. The first skeleton thus has the bar shape. The next loads, with
\code{stale = FALSE}, show the shape of the kind: \code{"bar"}, \code{"line"}, \code{"area"},
\code{"scatter"}, \code{"histogram"}, \code{"pie"}, \code{"heatmap"}, \code{"map"}, \code{"network"} or
\code{"wordcloud"}. With \code{remember}, the kind is also stored in the browser, so
on the next visit the first skeleton has that shape too. The first series
